(2696,2696,1,'Beyond the Overall Strength Index: Behavioral and Emotional Strength Profiles Among Kazakh Adolescents in Bayan-Ölgii Province, Mongolia','<p>Strength-based assessment can reveal competencies that are not captured by deficit-focused approaches, yet a global composite score may conceal lower strengths in specific subscales. This study examined behavioral and emotional strengths among 314 Kazakh adolescents (160 boys, 154 girls; ages 11–19 years) in Bayan-Ölgii Province, Mongolia, using a Kazakh-language administration of the Behavioral and Emotional Rating Scale–Second Edition (BERS-2). The mean Strength Index was 99.58 (SD = 15.56), and 72.9% of participants were classified as Average or higher. However, among adolescents with an Average-or-higher Strength Index, 33.6% had at least one Below Average or Poor subscale score. Internal consistency was good across the five subscales (α = .806–.859), although a correlated five-factor confirmatory model showed poor absolute fit, χ²(1264) = 5365.99, CFI = .536, TLI = .513, RMSEA = .105, and SRMR = .092. Parallel analysis suggested a more complex empirical structure. A MANCOVA showed a significant multivariate gender effect across the five subscales, Wilks’ Λ = .788, F(5, 307) = 16.51, p &lt; .001, whereas age was not significant. Within the Average-or-higher group, a higher Strength Index strongly reduced the odds of hidden subscale-specific vulnerability, OR = 0.14 per 10-point increase, 95% CI [0.08, 0.24]. These findings support subscale-level interpretation of strengths and indicate that the Kazakh-language BERS-2 requires further psychometric evaluation before validation can be claimed.</p>','["adolescents, behavioral and emotional strengths, BERS-2, Kazakh, Mongolia, strengths-based assessment"]',NULL,27,1,'01 - 15',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-02',1),
(2697,2697,1,'Structural-Adaptive and Resilience-Driven Hybrid Deep Learning Framework for Intrusion Detection in Autonomous Vehicular Networks','<p>Safety-critical choices depend on real-time V2V, V2I, and vehicle communications, therefore autonomous vehicular networks need intrusion detection systems with structural awareness, temporal sensitivity, and adversary drift resistance. Connected ECUs and edge intelligence have extended the cyber-attack surface, but most IDS frameworks are signature-driven, centrally taught, or data-centric. Distribution shift, adversarial perturbations, poor contextual reasoning, and limited scalability plague federated vehicular situations. In dynamic attack intensification sets, present systems prioritize classification accuracy over structural vulnerability propagation and cyber-resilience elasticity. A five-stage analytical approach for validated autonomous car network intrusion detection is described here. First, AVTC-CEGM constructs a structural assault surface topology vector from Temporal Causal Entropy Divergence to characterize communication instability. Spatio-Temporal Adversarial Drift Modeling using Physics-Aware Diffusion Transformers (STADM-PADT) calculates the Vehicular Adversarial Drift Coefficient with vehicular dynamics limitations. Third, HQ-GMNIR attributes contextual anomalies using coherence collapse analysis. Fourth, trust-entropy aggregation and reinforcement-based response control reduce latency and packet loss in FTT-POM. Finally, the HRV-CRBE formalizes multi-dataset stress resilience elasticity. A thorough analysis of vehicle and network intrusion datasets indicates improved detection accuracy (97%+), fewer false positives, faster federated convergence, and resilience to high Intensity attacks. The architecture turns IDS research from anomaly detection to structurally informed, adversarially robust, and system-level cyber-resilient intelligence for next-generation autonomous mobility.</p>','["Autonomous Vehicular Networks, Causal Entropy Graph Mining, Adversarial Drift Modeling, Federated Intrusion Detection, Cyber-Resilience Engineering, Analysis"]',NULL,27,1,'16 - 37','10.17762/jidmis.v3.2697','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-02',2),
(2698,2698,1,'Generative AI in the VFX Pipeline: Productivity Gains, Skill Shifts, and Ethical Challenges — An Indian Perspective','<p>Generative Artificial Intelligence (GAI) tools—including text-to-image diffusion models, AI-driven inpainting, automated rotoscoping, neural rendering, and compositing assistants—are rapidly integrating into global visual effects (VFX) pipelines. While international studios report workflow experimentation and partial automation gains, empirical evidence on productivity, role transformation, and ethical governance remains limited, particularly within India’s service-dominant VFX sector. This explanatory sequential mixed-methods study investigates the measurable productivity impacts, skill reallocation, and ethical challenges associated with generative AI adoption in Indian VFX studios. Phase I consists of a national survey (~200 respondents) measuring adoption rates, estimated time savings, task shifts, training needs, and governance practices. Phase II includes 15–20 semi-structured interviews and three in-depth case studies capturing pipeline changes, validation practices, and intellectual property (IP) considerations. Quantitative analysis employs descriptive statistics, paired t-tests, regression modeling, and factor analysis, while qualitative thematic coding provides cross-case synthesis. The study aims to (1) quantify productivity gains across tasks; (2) map evolving professional roles; (3) catalogue ethical and IP risks; and (4) propose a studio-level AI governance framework and reskilling roadmap. Findings will inform industry stakeholders, educators, and policymakers seeking responsible AI integration in VFX production.</p>','["Generative AI, Visual Effects, Productivity Analysis, Skill Transformation, Creative Industries, AI Ethics, Mixed-Methods Research"]',NULL,27,1,'38 - 44',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-02',3),
(2284,2284,1,'An Enhanced Triangle Algorithm for Large-Scale Support Vector Machine Optimization: A Comparative Study with Classical and Modern Solvers','<p>Support vector machines (SVMs) are established maximum-margin classifiers, but the quadratic-programming structure of SVM training can become computationally demanding when dimensionality and sample size increase. This article revisits the Triangle Algorithm, a geometry-based method that determines convex-hull intersection or separation and approximates the optimal supporting hyperplanes for the hard-margin SVM problem. The reorganised article presents an improved implementation perspective, which requires the same four implementation mechanisms from the source MATLAB study: closest-point updates of two line segments, caching of dot products, an anti-zig-zag pivot strategy, and prioritised searches over non-bounding points, but also introduces the published Triangle Algorithm. The original numerical tests are kept unchanged in order to ensure the tracking of the new journal to validated original results. The convex-hull intersection, dimensional scaling, and inter-hull distance sensitivity were examined using two randomly generated unit balls. The average number of iterations required for 10,000 dimensions and 26.53 s for 5,000 points in each set was 4.95 when doing intersection testing. Use of the Triangle Algorithm at 10,000 dimensions with optimal support took 36.39 s, whereas for Sequential Minimal Optimisation (SMO) this time was 165.99 s, with the reported support distances being almost equal in both cases. For increasing distance between convex hulls, Triangle Algorithm runtime decreased from 5.14 s to 2.745 s, while SMO remained in the range 67.014 s – 118.436 s. These results demonstrate the feasibility of geometry-based SVM optimisation, and inspire improved implementation methods. The approach is also placed in the current debate in comparison to LIBSVM, LIBLINEAR, ThunderSVM, stochastic subgradient, multilevel and modern large-scale kernel solvers.</p>','["Support vector machine", "Triangle Algorithm", "sequential minimal optimization", "convex hull", "large-scale optimization", "geometric optimization", "hard-margin classification"]',NULL,26,1,'01 - 15',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-27',1),
(2323,2323,1,'Strategic Performance Measurement Using the Balanced Scorecard in Digital Media Organizations: A Study of Organizational Effectiveness','<p>This paper investigates the use of Balanced Scorecard (BSC) as a strategic performance measurement instrument in the improvement of the organizational effectiveness in digital media houses. A quantitative research design was used and sampled 135 respondents by giving them a structured questionnaire using a structured approach. The study underlines the idea that the importance of non-financial performance indicators grows due to the digitally-friendly environment, which is marked by rapid innovation and customer-focus. Such results suggest that the Balanced Scorecard provides an overall framework of incorporating not only the strategic objectives but also operational performance and consequently improve the operations of decision-making in digital media organisations and sustainable competitive advantage.</p>','["Balanced Scorecard, Strategic Performance Measurement, Organizational Effectiveness, Digital Media Organizations, Customer Perspective, Learning and Growth, Digital Transformation"]',NULL,26,1,'16 - 23',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-27',2),
(2324,2324,1,'Evidence-Based Classroom Decision-Making through Educational Analytics: A Study among Secondary School Teachers','<p>Currently, educational analytics is becoming more of an approach in supporting for evidence-based classroom decision making because of its ability to apply educational data for enhancing teaching and learning. The study adopted the quantitative descriptive and analytical approach in order to examine the aspects of educational analytics for the secondary school teachers.A quantitative descriptive and analytical research design was involved in this study. A structured questionnaire was used and 300 secondary school teachers were sampled in this research by stratified random sampling technique to obtain primary data. A conclusion from the findings is that early learning teachers have a positive attitude toward educational analytics and see its potential in lesson planning, assessment of students and in monitoring the classroom. Hypothesis testing suggested that teacher training, institutional support, and experience in teaching have significant impact on effective use of educational analytics. The research findings indicate that improving digital infrastructure assets and professional development will have a positive impact on digital classroom effectiveness and digital instructional practices.</p>','["Educational Analytics, Evidence-Based Decision-Making, Classroom Decision-Making, Secondary School Teachers, Data-Driven Teaching, Learning Analytics."]',NULL,26,1,'24 - 35',NULL,'The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-27',3),
(1966,1966,1,'Sustainable Operations Management through Data-Driven Inventory Classification and Material Requirement Planning','<p>The increasing complexity of supply chains and the growing emphasis on sustainability have necessitated the development of more adaptive and data-driven inventory management approaches. The traditional inventory management systems fail to address the demand uncertainty, cost-effectiveness and resource utilization at the same time. The current paper will propose a hybrid concept of sustainable operations management that will be grounded in the integration of the ABC-XYZ classification of the inventories and optimization principle of Material Requirement Planning (MRP). The differentiation of inventory items occurred by applying economic value and demand variability with the set of 3,204 stock-keeping units, and thus, it was possible to apply various control strategies. These results reveal that the goods of high value and that has steady demand are concentrated in the major part and must be monitored first and planned properly. Demand variability is an inclusion in classification and it enhances the decision making by taking into consideration the uncertainty in the replenishment process. Besides, the optimization of safety stock and reorder point improves the inventory balance by reducing excess stock and stock out stock. Inventory turnover affects the business performance positively as observed in the analysis whereas holding cost affects the business performance negatively and the inventory should be used efficiently. The study is relevant to the existing literature as it brings to one platform classification, forecasting sensitivity, and optimization of planning. The findings provide practical insights for developing resilient and sustainable inventory systems in dynamic supply chain environments.</p>','["Inventory Optimization", "ABC\\u2013XYZ Classification", "Material Requirement Planning", "Demand Uncertainty", "Sustainable Supply Chain"]',NULL,25,1,'01-10','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1966','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',1),
(1967,1967,1,'Beyond Accounting on Capital Adequacy: Capital Quality, Capital Quantity, and Risk Density in Bank Resilience Evidence from the Jordanian Banking System','<p>This study examines how bank capital should be interpreted when a regulatory capital ratio combines economically distinct dimensions of balance-sheet strength. Using source-level financial-soundness files issued by the Central Bank of Jordan, the analysis constructs a bank-year panel for 2015-2019 and separates three components of capital strength: capital quality, measured as core or Tier 1 capital relative to total regulatory capital; capital quantity, measured primarily as total regulatory capital relative to total assets; and risk density, measured as risk-weighted assets relative to total assets. The validated descriptive sample contains 121 bank-year observations, and the one-year-ahead fixed-effects estimations contain 96 observations. Capital quality is exceptionally high and compressed, averaging 97.9% of total regulatory capital. In conventional bank and year fixed-effects models, capital quantity has larger point estimates and greater incremental explanatory content than capital quality, although small-cluster wild-bootstrap inference does not support a general claim of quantity dominance. The clearest result emerges from decomposing the conventional risk-weighted capital ratio. After controlling explicitly for risk-weighted-asset density, a one-within-bank-standard-deviation increase in capital quantity is associated with a 0.78 percentage-point reduction in next-year non-performing loans, with a wild-cluster-bootstrap p-value of 0.039. By contrast, the headline total-capital-to-risk-weighted-assets ratio changes sign and provides little robust predictive information, indicating that denominator variation can materially alter the economic interpretation of capital adequacy. Formal nonlinear tests reject a purely linear treatment of capital quality and identify turning regions around a core-capital share of 97%, although the estimated shape does not imply a simple monotonic saturation mechanism. Interaction tests provide no evidence that capital quality attenuates the NPL-profitability relationship, while the positive association between capital quantity and future profitability weakens as risk density rises. The evidence supports a three-part supervisory interpretation of capital strength: capital composition, capital intensity relative to total assets, and RWA density should be assessed jointly rather than inferred from a single headline ratio.</p>','["bank capital", "capital quality", "capital quantity", "Tier 1 capital", "risk-weighted assets", "bank resilience", "non-performing loans", "Basel III", "Jordan"]',NULL,25,1,'11-38','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1967','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',2),
(1968,1968,1,'Assessing Trust in Conversational Agents through Sentiment, Emotion, Response Quality, Latency, and Engagement - A Mixed Synthetic and Human-Intervened Dataset Study','<p>In this agentic era, trust in conversational agents plays a critical role in their adoption and effectiveness across diverse applications. This research focuses on assessing trust in conversational agents by analyzing the impact of sentiment, emotion, response quality, latency, and user engagement. Synthetic and human-intervened datasets were generated using a range of proprietary and open-source large language models (LLMs), including GPT-4, Claude, Llama, Gemini, and others. A total of 3,909 conversations with 26,329 turns were created, simulating diverse conversational scenarios. Trust evaluations were conducted by trained human annotators (n=15) using a standardized framework, scoring perceived competence, benevolence, integrity, and overall trustworthiness on a 1–7 scale. Inter-annotator agreement was established with Cohen’s Kappa coefficients (target κ &gt; 0.75). Sentiment analysis and emotion detection were employed to derive trust metrics, while contextual factors influencing trust judgments were documented qualitatively. The findings reveal that sentiment and emotion significantly influence trust perceptions, while response latency impacts user satisfaction. Engagement patterns, such as conversation length and emotional variation, were found to correlate with trust levels. The research also developed a modular codebase to support reproducible experimentation and extensibility, enabling integration of new models and metrics. This study provides insights into designing trustworthy conversational agents and contributes to the broader field of trust engineering by offering methodologies for quantifying and optimizing trust in AI systems.</p>','["Comparative Sentiment Analysis", "Public Opinion", "Stunting", "K-Nearest Neighbor", "Na\\u00efve Bayes", "Decision Tree."]',NULL,25,1,'39-58','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1968','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',3),
(1596,1596,1,'Performance Comparison of K-Means and Expectation-Maximization Clustering Algorithms for Network Intrusion Detection','<p>The tremendous growth of networks and internet-based services have led to more numerous and complicated cyberattacks. Organizations today widely adopt networks for many things; like sending messages, transferring money, data storing, and using internet for communication and cloud computing. Therefore, networks are the attractive targets for the cyber-attacks and require protective mechanisms. Firewalls, antivirus program, and access controls provide the preventative means, but they are only enough for new attacks or simple kinds of attacks, they cannot provide any protection to sophisticated or newly discovered kinds of attacks. Intrusion detection systems are an unavoidable system for a cybersecurity environment, since they continuously monitor network traffic and detect malicious activity that the prevention mechanisms miss out. Machine learning methods have gained a considerable success at the field of network intrusion detection since they can analyse and learn patterns from network traffic, unlike rule-based systems, they do not require predefined signature of attacks. There are two types of machine learning algorithms, supervised learning and unsupervised learning. Unsupervised learning is more efficient for network intrusion detection and particularly Clustering as a type of unsupervised learning is used since it can help in partitioning the network traffic into various types of connection with identifying the anomalous connection without using the pre-labelled intrusion training data. The most popular methods in clustering are the K-means algorithm for its high calculation efficiency and ease of implementation, while expectation-maximization (EM) algorithms provide probabilistic model based-clustering which can model more complex kind of data. Only very few research has compared the two types of clustering, K-Means and EM algorithms for network intrusion detection system under exactly the same experimental settings and the same common dataset, The present study outlines the comparative analysis of K-Means and Expectation-Maximization clustering algorithms towards network intrusion detection through applying on the KDD Cup 1999 benchmark dataset. Experiments is setup using WEKA data mining tool for having a consistent research environment. Comparison research based on the computationally performance, clustering patterns, converging behaviours, and the execution efficiency in general manner. Data for network traffic is pre-processed and then the two clustering algorithms are analysed using exactly same parameters where appropriate. Comparison metrics for two algorithms includes the runtime, distribution patterns across clusters, speed of convergence, and clustering validity. The primary research intention is to assess the strengths and weakness of the K-means and EM algorithms for an unsupervised detection of intrusions, thus providing a guide for an appropriate clustering technique for large networks information security system.</p>','["Intrusion Detection System, Network Security, Machine Learning, Unsupervised Learning, K-Means Clustering, Expectation-Maximization, WEKA, KDD Cup 1999, Cybersecurity, Data Mining."]',NULL,24,1,'01-14','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1596','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',1),
(1615,1615,1,'A Novel Iterative Machine Learning-Driven Framework for Reliable and Adaptive Cloud Data Migration','<p>Cloud data migration has become an essential requirement for modern enterprises due to the rapid growth of cloud computing and data-intensive applications. However, achieving reliable and efficient migration remains challenging because of heterogeneous data types, fluctuating network bandwidth, diverse migration tools, and the need to preserve data consistency throughout the migration process. Most existing approaches evaluate migration tools independently, assume static network conditions, and provide limited support for adaptive optimization and verifiable migration outcomes. This paper presents AMF-CloudForge, a unified machine learning-driven framework that integrates migration state analysis, intelligent scheduling, consistency preservation, and real-time adaptive management into a single end-to-end architecture. The proposed framework begins with Polytype Migration State Encoding (PMSE), which performs comprehensive micro-benchmarking across different data types, file sizes, and network conditions to generate Migration State Tensors and Tool Efficiency Scores that accurately characterize migration behavior. These learned representations are then utilized by the Topology-Aware Diffusion Scheduler (TADS) to optimize chunking, routing, and migration tool selection by considering dynamic network topology, bandwidth variations, and transfer costs. To ensure data integrity and consistency, the Causal Delta with Erasure-coded Commitment (CDEC) module combines causal dependency tracking, adaptive erasure coding, and cryptographic quorum verification to guarantee reliable and verifiable data commits without data loss or metadata inconsistencies. Furthermore, the Uncertainty-Quantified Twin Supervisor (UQTS) continuously monitors the migration process through a probabilistic digital twin, enabling risk-aware scheduling adjustments and real-time Service Level Objective (SLO) verification under changing system conditions.Together, these components form a fully automated migration framework capable of policy-driven execution and machine-verifiable migration reporting. Experimental evaluation on heterogeneous cloud workloads demonstrates that the proposed framework reduces overall migration makespan by 35–45%, sustains effective bandwidth utilization above 85%, and limits unrecoverable data loss to approximately 10⁻¹¹ per 10 TB of migrated data. By integrating machine learning-based performance modeling with adaptive scheduling, cryptographic consistency verification, and intelligent runtime supervision, the proposed framework transforms cloud data migration from a static, tool-centric process into a reliable, adaptive, and continuously optimized cloud service.</p>','["Cloud Data Migration, Machine Learning Scheduling, Causal Consistency, Erasure Coding, Automated Framework, Process."]',NULL,24,1,'15-38','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1615','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',2),
(1616,1616,1,'AI-Powered Fraud Detection in Banking: A Big Data Analytics Perspective','<p>Banking fraud presents a persistent challenge in the digital era, with financial losses continuing to escalate as transaction volumes grow exponentially. This paper examines AI-enabled fraud detection frameworks that leverage big data analytics, machine learning, and deep learning methodologies. The review systematically categorizes existing approaches across supervised learning, anomaly detection, ensemble methods, and deep neural network architectures. Special attention is directed toward hybrid frameworks that combine multiple techniques to address extreme class imbalance, concept drift, and real-time processing constraints. Analysis indicates that ensemble models incorporating XGBoost with LSTM networks achieve accuracy exceeding 98% with substantially reduced false positive rates. Critical challenges including model interpretability, data privacy, and deployment scalability are examined, alongside promising directions for future research in this domain.</p>','["Big data analytics", "banking fraud detection", "machine learning", "deep learning", "ensemble methods", "anomaly detection", "hybrid models."]',NULL,24,1,'39-51','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1616','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',3),
(1370,1370,1,'The Effect of Lean Manufacturing on Customer Satisfaction through Service Quality','<p>Purpose – The aim of current research is to identify service quality as a mediator variable between lean manufacturing and customer satisfaction of the state company of Textile &amp; Leather Industries in Iraq.</p>
INSERT INTO publications (id,submission_id,version,title,abstract,keywords,references_text,issue_id,section_id,pages,doi,copyright_holder,copyright_year,license_url,status,date_published,seq) VALUES
(1070,1070,1,'An Adaptive Cybersecurity Framework Integrating Machine Learning, Zero Trust Policy, and Blockchain for Academic Cloud Environments','<p>Cloud-based academic environments such as Learning Management Systems (LMS), Open Journal Systems (OJS), institutional repositories, and web applications face increasing cybersecurity challenges due to heterogeneous users, distributed services, and extensive exposure to public networks. Existing security approaches remain fragmented, where machine learning focuses on threat detection, Zero Trust Architecture (ZTA) emphasizes access control, and blockchain is primarily used for secure logging. The lack of integration among these components limits the ability of security systems to adapt dynamically to evolving cyber threats. This study proposes an Adaptive Cybersecurity Framework (ACF) that integrates unsupervised machine learning-based anomaly detection, a risk-based Zero Trust Policy Engine, and blockchain-based immutable audit logging within a continuous adaptive feedback loop. The framework was evaluated using 450,000 anonymized HTTP and Web Application Firewall (WAF) events collected from a multi-domain academic cloud environment consisting of LMS, OJS, repositories, and supporting web applications. The analysis revealed structured and repetitive attack behaviors dominated by automated endpoint probing and cross-domain propagation patterns, indicating ecosystem-level security threats. The proposed risk assessment mechanism demonstrated effective alignment between anomaly detection and policy-based decision making. Experimental results achieved an AUROC of 0.7296 for risk-based threat detection while maintaining an average decision latency of approximately 11 ms, indicating suitability for real-time deployment. Blockchain integration further provided verifiable, tamper-resistant audit trails for mitigation actions and policy enforcement activities. This study contributes an ecosystem-aware adaptive cybersecurity paradigm that bridges threat detection, policy enforcement, and auditability through a unified security architecture for Academic Cloud Environments.</p>','["cybersecurity", "machine learning", "zero trust", "blockchain", "academic cloud", "anomaly detection."]',NULL,21,1,'53 - 93','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1070','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',3),
(993,993,1,'Comparative Analysis of User Sentiment towards Public Opinion on Stunting using K-Nearest Neighbor, Naive Bayes, and Decision Tree Algorithm','<p>Stunting is a critical public health issue, particularly in developing countries, with long-term impacts on physical and cognitive development due to chronic malnutrition. In the digital era, public sentiment, and opinion on stunting, as reflected on social media platforms, can provide valuable insights for policymakers and healthcare providers. However, there has been limited research combining multiple machine-learning algorithms for sentiment analysis on this topic. This study aims to conduct a comparative analysis of user sentiment on X regarding public opinion on stunting using three widely utilized text classification algorithms: K-Nearest Neighbour (KNN), Naïve Bayes, and Decision Tree. The research involves collecting and annotating sentiment data (positive, negative, neutral) from social media platforms like Twitter and X, followed by model training and evaluation using metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. Results from the analysis of 1227 data points (2023-2024) with an 80:20 train-test split revealed that Naïve Bayes achieved the highest accuracy at 87.18%, followed by Decision Tree at 79.83%, and KNN at 57.56%. Meanwhile, a comparison using a 70:30 train-test split yielded similar results, with Naïve Bayes achieving 86.41%, Decision Tree 78.41%, and KNN 54.48%. The study demonstrates the effectiveness of the Naïve Bayes algorithm for sentiment analysis in public health contexts, offering a novel approach to understanding public opinion on stunting. By leveraging social media data, this research provides real-time insights that can guide policy and public health interventions. Furthermore, the comparative analysis of algorithms provides valuable knowledge on the most effective models for sentiment analysis in complex, sentiment-driven public health issues. The study emphasizes the role of machine learning in addressing malnutrition and stunting through data-driven policy decisions.</p>','["Comparative Sentiment Analysis", "Public Opinion", "Stunting", "K-Nearest Neighbor", "Na\\u00efve Bayes", "Decision Tree."]',NULL,20,1,'01 - 20','10.59543/jidmis.v3.993','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',1),
(994,994,1,'The Impact of AI-Based Applications on Payroll Management and Recruitment Processes in Private Universities and Colleges in Oman','<p>Artificial Intelligence (AI) is one of the most versatile innovations to date and plays a major role within the world of business. It made major changes to organization management of daily activities in terms of tasks automation, processing large amounts of information, and the enhancement of accuracy and efficiency. Within Human Resource Management and Finance sector, AI has significantly made positive impact particularly upon recruitment and payroll management. AI as technology saves time, decreases errors and improves informed decisions of organizations.<br/>AI-based systems are common in the application of handling payroll calculations, identifying qualified candidates and ascertain compliance related to taxation and compensation policies. AI also provides comprehensive perspectives for managers to craft improved strategic decisions, increase transparency and aggregate work performance by the employees. However, organizations still face challenges despite the advantages during the application of AI, which include algorithms bias, high costs of implementation, privacy risk and resistance being shown by employees due to the setup of new systems.<br/>This study focuses on private universities and colleges in Oman, such as Majan University College, Mazoon College, Al Sharqiyah University, and Middle East College, etc., because these institutions have well-structured HR departments that manage complex recruitment, payroll, and employee management processes. Private higher education institutions in Oman are increasingly adopting digital technologies to improve efficiency, transparency, and decision-making within HR functions. Focusing on these universities and colleges allows the study to examine real-world HR practices, assess the impact of AI on recruitment and payroll activities, and understand how AI can support fairness, efficiency, and sustainable development within the work environment.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence (AI), Payroll Management, Recruitment Processes"]',NULL,20,1,'21 - 35','10.59543/jidmis.v3.994','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',2),
(995,995,1,'Municipal Greenhouse Gas Inventory as a Decision-Support Tool for Climate Governance: Evidence from an Agricultural Municipality in the Philippines','<p>Local governments play a pivotal role in achieving national climate commitments; however, many rural municipalities lack evidence-based greenhouse gas (GHG) inventories to guide climate mitigation planning. This study evaluates the greenhouse gas emission profile of Rizal, Kalinga, Philippines, and demonstrates how municipal-scale inventories can strengthen evidence-based local climate governance through the enhanced Local Climate Change Action Plan (eLCCAP). A descriptive-analytical research design was employed using primary data collected from 744 respondents across households, farms, commercial establishments, institutions, and fuel suppliers, supplemented by municipal records and national activity datasets. Emissions were quantified using the Philippine Greenhouse Gas Inventory Quantification Support Spreadsheet following IPCC-based accounting protocols for Scope 1, Scope 2, and Scope 3 emissions. Results indicate that total municipal emissions reached 7,877.66 tCO₂e, with Scope 1 emissions accounting for 63.13%, Scope 2 for 36.20%, and Scope 3 for 0.67%. Agriculture was identified as the dominant emission sector, primarily driven by livestock and agricultural soils, followed by household and transport energy use and refrigeration and air-conditioning systems. Despite limited industrial activity, indirect electricity consumption represented a substantial share of total emissions, while existing carbon sequestration partially offset agricultural emissions. The inventory further enabled the identification of priority mitigation pathways including climate-smart agriculture, renewable energy adoption, energy-efficient cooling technologies, improved waste management, and institutional strengthening. The study demonstrates that municipal greenhouse gas inventories provide an effective decision-support framework for integrating localized emission evidence into climate policy, enabling rural local governments to prioritize cost-effective mitigation strategies while enhancing resilience and contributing to national decarbonization goals. These findings provide a replicable framework for climate governance in agricultural municipalities across developing countries.</p>','["Greenhouse gas inventory", "Climate governance", "Local climate change action plan", "Municipal emissions", "Climate-smart agriculture", "Low-carbon development"]',NULL,20,1,'36 - 51','10.59543/jidmis.v3.995','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',3),
(781,781,1,'An Integrated Entropy–TOPSIS and Decision-Tree Framework for Operational Priority Setting in a University-Affiliated Hotel: Evidence from the PSU Golden Lion Hotel, Philippines','<p>The structure of a University-affiliated hotel, reflecting its dual commercial–pedagogical mandate, makes prioritising improvements to the hotel''s operational processes, based on discipline and scientific evidence, more complex and significant than in other types of hotels. The study builds on this work to create an integrated decision analytic framework to turn evidence from multiple stakeholder surveys into a defensible prioritization of operational improvements and applies this framework at the Pangasinian State University (PSU) Golden Lion Hotel in the Philippines. Eight dimensions of operation were rated by 400 respondents who were managers, staff/interns, and guests. The performance gap, derived importance (with respect to overall guest satisfaction) and the percentage of dissatisfied evaluations are used to fill a decision matrix, which is ranked objectively using Shannon entropy and classified objectively using TOPSIS, and complemented by the importance–performance analysis and a CART classification model. The dominant improvement priority (closeness coefficient 0.866) is service quality, and the CART model achieves a ten-fold cross-validated accuracy of 91.0% while using only about 98.0% of the predictive information for this priority, as compared to the modal baseline of 52.5%. The weakest performing dimensions are sustainability practices (weighted mean 1.89) and technology integration (2.17), and they are both ranked second and third, respectively, based on equal criterion weights. It provides a transparent framework for hotel decision makers with a replicable pipeline from stakeholder perceptions to an operational investment ordering, and it adds an application of objective-weight multi-criteria decision-making and machine learning in the relatively under-studied university-hotel field.</p>','["multi-criteria decision-making", "TOPSIS", "entropy weighting", "decision tree", "hotel op-erations management", "service quality."]',NULL,19,1,'01 - 19','10.59543/jidmis.v3.781','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',1),
(782,782,1,'An Ensemble Machine Learning Model for Mastitis Disease Identification in Cows','<p>The worldwide dairy sector faces a major problem from mastitis, a disease that affects dairy cows widely and has a substantial financial effect. Timely and precise diagnosis is essential for efficient treatment and to stop the impact to a larger extent. Machine learning proven to be one of the assisted technologies for the identification of mastitis in cows and other cattle in an effective and precise manner. In this paper we have presented an ensemble model for mastitis identification. First, we implement outlier elimination and data anomaly detection based on the Implement Quartile Range (IRQ). The Box-Cox transformation, which normalizes the data distribution and makes it more appropriate for algorithms that assume a normal distribution, is then used to resolve skewness in numerical characteristics. Next for feature extraction three techniques has been applied namely Principal Component Analysis (PCA), Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA) and Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE). Also SMOTE has been used to balance the data later on. Then proposed ensemble model based on LR, DT, RF, GB, NB, Extra Tree and XGBoost has been tested on the data based on 5-fold and 3-fold cross -validation. Also, comparative evaluation has been done based on accuracy, precision, recall, F1 score, training and validation Loss for the proposed approach with respect to the existing methods.</p>','["Mastitis", "SVM", "RF", "KNN", "DT", "GB", "PCA", "ExtraTree", "XGBoost", "SDG"]',NULL,19,1,'20 - 42','10.59543/jidmis.v3.782','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',2),
(783,783,1,'Early Prediction of Neurological Disorders using Automatic Deep Feature Extraction and Machine Learning','<p>The focus of recent research has been on using advanced computer-aided diagnostic (CAD) techniques and a variety of modalities to identify neurological disorders. Important and possibly deadly conditions, neurological diseases such as Alzheimer''s disease (AD), stroke, epilepsy, Parkinson''s disease (PD), cerebral palsy, autism, and schizophrenia (SZ) often result in more serious health issues. CAD systems for neurological diseases using a variety of modalities have attracted a lot of interest due to different approaches of Artificial Intelligence (AI) like Deep Learning (DL) and Machine Learning (ML). However, the difficulty of automatically predicting many neurological disorders using neuroimaging data remains unresolved due to the lack of a comprehensive and highly accurate solution. A novel approach is presented in which an automated deep feature extraction along with machine learning classifiers were used for detection neurological diseases (for instance schizophrenia, Parkinson''s disease, and Alzheimer''s disease) from brain scans at early stages. In order to identify neurological disorders various methods use neuroimaging studies, mainly magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). Preprocessing MRI scans, data augmentation, deep feature extraction, and machine learning-based classification are all included in the suggested model. Image filtering and contrast augmentation techniques are applied to the acquired raw MRI images to improve their quality. Various methods are used to enhance the categorization accuracy of the current datasets. For automated deep feature extraction from input MRI pictures, we modified the Convolutional Neural Network''s (CNN) layers. Deep feature extraction lowers the dimensionality and computing needs of an updated CNN by extracting complex properties from its deeper levels. The effectiveness of the suggested model for classifying neurological disorders is assessed using sophisticated machine learning classifiers. The suggested method is better at detecting neurological issues, according to experimental research on neurological illnesses and imaging modalities.</p>','["Alzheimer\\u2019s Disease", "Artificial Intelligence", "Convolutional Neural Network", "Machine Learning, Neurological Disorders", "Neuroimaging"]',NULL,19,1,'43 - 69','10.59543/jidmis.v3.783','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-23',3),
(594,594,1,'Two-Stage 3D U-Net-Based Segmentation Of Maxillofacial Structures From CBCT Scans For Dental Implant Planning','<p>This work represents a 3D segmentation framework for automatic landmark delineation in Cone Beam Computed Tomography to aid dental implant planning. The proposed method aimed to segment important anatomical details like bone, nerve canals, and teeth using the Bone segmentation algorithm, Deep Neural Network Based Minimal Medical segmentation, and Tooth segmentation method, respectively. The bone segmentation algorithm utilizes two-stage 3D U-Net architecture where coarse global segmentation combines with fine segmentation for accurate detection of anatomical structures like tooth, alveolar ridge, maxillary sinus and nasal cavity. Preprocessing of CBCT images is done preceding to segmentation to remove poisonous noise using PNR algorithm, Thermal noise using TFR algorithm and ring artifacts with RAR algorithm. The regions of interest identified in segmentation phase leads to exact measurements of important anatomical structures that further assists in automation of medical report generation system. The assessment of segmentation algorithms is done using parameters such as DICE-Score and IoU. Our proposed system achieved 0.96, 0.97, 0.92, 0.96 and 0.99 DICE-Score values for mandible, maxilla, nerve canal, teeth and background respectively as well as 0.93, 0.94, 0.86, 0.93 and 0.99 IoU score values for mandible, maxilla, nerve canal, teeth and background respectively.</p>','["Dental Implant planning", "CBCT", "3D U-Net", "Segmentation", "Medical Imaging", "Deep learning", "Tooth segmentation", "Bone segmentation", "Machine Learning"]',NULL,18,1,'01 - 25','10.59543/jidmis.v3.594','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',1),
(595,595,1,'AI-Driven Detection, Segmentation, and Classification of Medicinal Plant Leaves Using Computer Vision and Deep Learning','<p>Ayurvedic medicines play a significant role in maintaining the physical and mental health of people. Currently, these medicines are widely adopted to cure various diseases. Therefore, precise detection and classification of medicinal crops are critical for ensuring effective treatments. However, the scarcity of experts in this field poses a challenge, making the process of detecting and classifying herbal plants both complex and time-consuming. To address this issue, a fully automatic system for medicinal plant classification is much required. Currently, Computer Vision (CV) and Machine Learning (ML)/Deep Learning (DL) based systems have gained attention to provide automated detection and classification solutions for these types of problems i.e. detection, segmentation and classification. Therefore, this study adopts this concept and proposes a comprehensive framework for medicinal plant leaf classification, integrating state-of-the-art DL and feature extraction techniques. Initially, data augmentation methods such as rotation, flipping, scaling, and color jittering are applied to enhance dataset diversity and improve model generalization. In next stage, image segmentation task is applied by utilizing advanced deep learning Yolov8 mode which helps to accurately extract leaf regions from complex backgrounds. The segmented images are then analysed to extract features using pre-trained VGG16 deep learning models, which are further enriched with handcrafted texture, color, and shape features to ensure robust representation. These diverse feature sets are fused and classified using an ensemble of ML classifiers, including Random Forest (RF), Support Vector Machine (SVM), and Gradient Boosting (GB). The proposed system achieves classification accuracies of 87.65%, 83.20%, and 89.32% for RF, SVM, and GB, respectively. Experimental results demonstrate the system''s effectiveness in achieving good accuracy and reliability in medicinal plant leaf classification. This approach provides a significant step toward automating plant identification, promoting the efficient use of Ayurvedic medicinal resources, and reducing dependency on domain experts.</p>','["Medicinal Plant Leaf detection, segmentation, classification, deep learning, computer vision."]',NULL,18,1,'26 - 40','10.59543/jidmis.v3.595','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',2),
(629,629,1,'A Deep Learning and YOLO-Based Hybrid Framework for Accurate Classification and Region-of-Interest Detection of Maize Leaf Diseases','<p>Maize leaf diseases are also major causes of loss in the productivity of the crop, and they are also visually similar to each other, which frequently causes a misclassification under manual inspection. The paper fills this gap of the necessity of a practical and automated solution by coming up with a hybrid of deep learning frameworks with the ability to not only classify diseases with high accuracy but also localize region of interest (ROI) with high precision. The aim is to strengthen the early detection with an integrated system that is comprised of both the advanced models of transfer learning and the YOLO-based detection. The dataset is processed systematically to enhance normalization, preprocessing, and augmentation of the dataset to enhance its strength in a variety of imaging conditions. Several deep learning models VGG16, ResNet50, InceptionV3 and MobileNetV2 and lightweight and ensemble versions were tested. The results indicate that Ensemble Model 1 (VGG16 + MobileNetV2) performs the best, with the accuracy of 96% and the precision, recall, and F1-score of 95%, 95%, and 95%, respectively, being higher than individual classifiers and Ensemble Model 2 do. The ability to mix complementary feature extraction tasks of deep and lightweight networks is seen to be powerful in this superior performance. YOLO component is highly effective in detecting diseased regions on the leaf with high confidence identified and produces clear and understandable bound-box results. Results provide validation that combining classification and object detection has benefits on the diagnostic reliability and decision-making capability which can be used for actionable decisions in agriculture. The area of their activity is also the real-time deployment, applicability to cross crops, and integration with mobile or IoT-based smart farming systems, which allows extending the disease management solutions to scale and efficiency.</p>','["Deep Learning Classification, Ensemble Model Optimization, YOLO ROI Detection, Maize Leaf Disease Analysis, Smart Agriculture Systems"]',NULL,18,1,'41 - 67','10.59543/jidmis.v3.629','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-14',3),
(423,423,1,'Deep Reinforcement Learning Driven LiNbO3 Dual Parallel Mach-Zehnder Modulator for Photonic THz-ISAC Systems','<p>The last few years have witnessed exponential rise in Terahertz integrated sensing and communication (THz-ISAC) systems serving an array of applications across industries. However, guaranteeing error-free transmission under diverse channel conditions remains a challenge that becomes even severe over THz transmission. Despite the fact that dual-parallel Mach-Zehnder modulator (DP-MZM) plays decisive towards THz-ISAC systems, inappropriate drive voltage and bias voltage can impact bit-error-rate (BER), intensity and group delay performance. To address it, this research proposes a novel multi-reward deep reinforcement learning (DRL) driven Ge-LiNbO3 heterogeneously integrated DP-MZM modulator architecture for THz-ISAC systems. The proposed Ge-LiNbO3 opto-electronic modulator makes use of linear frequency modulation-quadrature amplitude modulation (LFM-QAM) waveforms that enables high-data-rate communication while maintaining millimeter-level sensing resolution within the THz frequency spectrum. It leverages DP-MZM, integrated LFM-QAM and Ge-LiNbO3, where the first enabled BER reduction, while later with the adaptive waveforms improves sensing resolution performance. Ge-LiNbO3 improves THz efficiency and polarization control to meet ISAC communication demands. To achieve it, a robust multi-DRL framework is proposed that exploits state space characteristics including MZM voltage, graphene Fermi level, channel conditions, chirp-bandwidth, and QAM-order to update action vector (say, control vector) by updating drive voltage, bias voltage, chirp-bandwidth, chirp-rate reconfiguration, continuously. To meet target performance the proposed DRL model applied multi-reward function embodying BER, error-vector-magnitude (EVM), intensity and group delay information. Designing a robust opto-electronic modulator with Ge-LiNbO3 plasmonic characteristics, channel simulation and multi-reward DRL-based control vector optimization (i.e., drive voltage, bias voltage and wave characteristics (modulation order, fermi-level and chirp bandwidth)), the proposed model was tested under the different signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) conditions. Simulations confirmed that the proposed Ge-LiNbO3 heterogeneously integrated DP-MZM modulator achieves more than 300 Gbps transmission while maintaining sensing resolution less than 2 mm. Additionally, it showed 30%-35% improved efficiency gains, 25% reduced BER depicting its robustness towards run-time THz-ISAC communication under mobility. Depth performance characterization revealed that the proposed Ge-LiNbO3 DP-MZM modulator outperforms traditional LiNbO3 in terms of reduced error vector magnitude (EVM) (60%), improved output intensity (15%) and reduced BER performance under the diverse range of SNR (10-40 dB). The overall results and allied inferences confirm suitability of the proposed multi-reward DRL-driven Ge-LiNbO3 DP-MZM modulator for 6G THz-ISAC communication systems.</p>','["THz-ISAC systems, Opto-Electronic Modulator, Deep-Reinforcement Learning, Graphene Plasmonic, Ge-LiNbO3 Hybrid Material Modulator."]',NULL,16,1,'01 - 29','10.59543/jidmis.v3.423','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',1),
(424,424,1,'Deep Learning-Based Prediction of Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease Severity and Progression Using Respiratory Sounds','<p>Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) is a progressive lung disease with over 300 million getting effected worldwide. The accuracy of traditional methods of diagnosis relies on the expertise of the doctor. As technology progresses, with help of artificial intelligence the accuracy of diagnosis is further improved. But accurate detection of the sub-stages of COPD is still a major challenge. This work proposes a model to classify COPD into its sub categories based on GOLD staging with an accuracy of 83.33%. Further the work also predicts the chances of the individual’s disease progression to the next higher stage using four expert models namely COPD0-1, COPD1-2, COPD2-3, and COPD3-4. The accuracy of these expert models are 87.5%, 90.00%, 90.48% and 61.90% respectively. The proposed work sets a new benchmark in diagnosing COPD and predicting its progression to the next higher stage. This will help in better treatment to the individual with better patient outcomes.</p>','["COPD classification, COPD severity prediction, CONV2D, expert model, deep learning for COPD prediction"]',NULL,16,1,'30 - 42','10.59543/jidmis.v3.424','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',2),
(425,425,1,'The Deep Learning Based Segmentation And Multi-Classification of Skin Lesions Using Grab Cut with Flood Filling','<p>An expansion of atypical cell development is generated by various chronic variations as well as genetic disorders. The skin cancer is usually detected optically by medical screening through dermoscopic determination. Though, automated skin lesion segmentation and classification is difficult due to the visual correlation among benign as well as melanoma lesions. In this research, a Grab cut segmentation with Flood Filling (FF) is proposed for an effective segmentation and multi-classification of skin lesions. The lesion image datasets named HAM10000 and ISIC2019 are utilized to estimate a performance of a proposed method. The pre-processing techniques of Hair removal and Noise removal is used for achieve the better performance on the classification results. The DenseNet-169 architecture is utilized for the feature extraction process and Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) is utilized for a classification process. The classification algorithm classifies the input images as various types of skin lesion such as Melanoma (MEL), Melanocytic Nevi (MN), Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC), Actinic Keratosis (AK) and Benign Keratosis (BKL). The results shows that the proposed delivers performance metrics like accuracy, sensitivity, precision and F1-score of values about 98.89%, 99.38%, 98.01%, 97.28% and 98.85% respectively, which ensures better results compared with the existing method of ResNet50 CNN with SVM</p>','["Convolutional Neural Network, DenseNet-169, multi-classification, segmentation and skin lesion classification."]',NULL,16,1,'43 - 56','10.59543/jidmis.v3.425','The Authors',2026,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-06-30',3),
(182,182,1,'A Comparative Analysis of Transformer Architectures for Automated Lung Cancer Detection in CT Images','<p>The imperative for early-stage lung cancer detection is widely recognized as a critical determinant of therapeutic efficacy and patient survival. Conventional diagnostic workflows, however, are frequently constrained by their labour-intensive nature and susceptibility to interpretive inaccuracies, positioning artificial intelligence (AI) as a transformative technology in medical imaging. This research conducts a rigorous comparative analysis of four prominent vision transformer (ViT) architectures; Swin-Base, ViT-Base, DeiT-Base, and BEiT-Base evaluating their performance in the automated classification of lung cancer from computed tomography (CT) scans. The empirical validation was performed on the open-access IQ-OTH/NCCD dataset, a corpus of 1,097 images distributed across benign (n=120), malignant (n=561), and normal (n=416) classes. Model proficiency was quantified using established metrics of accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. The findings unequivocally establish the superiority of the Swin-Base model, which, by utilizing its innovative hierarchical design and shifted-window mechanism, attained a benchmark accuracy of 98.80% and an F1-score of 97.52%. While its counterparts achieved commendable accuracies ViT-Base (95.18%), DeiT-Base (96.39%), and BEiT-Base (95.78%) they did not match the performance of Swin-Base. Notably, this leading performance was achieved with greater computational efficiency, requiring a lower GFLOPS count than competing models.</p>
